A: After a long discussion, here is how these contracts may look:

[PexMethod, PexAllowedException(typeof(ArgumentOutOfRangeException))]
public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int index) 
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_index = index;
    int Old_x = (int) (arrList[index]);
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(Old_x);
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(Old_x);
    ...
}

[PexMethod, PexAllowedException(typeof(ArgumentOutOfRangeException))]
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int index) 
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    ...
}


"PUT_RemoveAtContract" and "PUT_GetContract" methods may throw "ArgumentOutOfRangeException" if you try to get element out of valid range of indexes.

As @Raj said, "PUT_ContainsContract" should have a precondition where it checks that if "x" is not in the array list, then the result of the "LastIndexOf(x)" should be -1. So the assertion would be:

Debug.Assert(arrList.LastIndexOf(x) == -1 || arrList.Contains(x));

Hope it helps,
-P

